<Base lang="en" title="eDistrict Form PDF: Every Certificate Form and the Declaration — in One Place" description="Where to get the eDistrict form PDF — the official source and JPEG rule for the self-declaration form, the real form arrangement for income, caste, domicile, birth, death, EWS and parivar register applications, and how to avoid form mistakes." path="/en/blog/edistrict-forms-download/" altPath="/blog/edistrict-forms-download/" faqs={faqs} updated="15 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-15" dateModified="2026-11-15">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">eDistrict Form PDF: Which Form Comes From Where — the Full List in One Place</h1>
<p>Most people searching “eDistrict form PDF” are stuck on one question — where do I download a separate form for each certificate? The truth is that “forms” in the eDistrict system are of two kinds. The first is the <strong>self-declaration form</strong> — a printed format that has to be filled, signed and uploaded with almost every application. The second is each service’s <strong>own application form</strong> — and that is not a PDF at all; it is filled online on the eSathi portal. This article puts the whole arrangement in one place: which paper-form goes with which service, where it comes from, in what format it is uploaded, and which mistakes stall an application. Never take a form from an unknown website — an old or wrong format is a cause of rejection at verification.</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/edistrict-forms-download-featured.webp" alt="All eDistrict UP forms and declarations — listed in one place (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<section class="box"><h2>The most important form — the self-declaration</h2><p>The one paper form that is compulsorily attached to most certificate applications — income, caste, domicile included — is the <strong>Self-Declaration Form</strong>. In it, the applicant declares the truth of their own details — name, address, income, caste and so on — with their signature. It is the simple alternative to a notary affidavit; it does not need to be notarised.</p><p><strong>Where to get this form:</strong> it can be downloaded from the official portal, <strong>edistrict.up.gov.in</strong>. Often, when you choose the concerned certificate service, the declaration format is also made available during the eSathi application process itself. For practice, our <a href="/en/self-declaration-form/">Self-Declaration Form guide</a> carries an unofficial sample PDF — but attach only the official portal’s format in a real application.</p><p><strong>Rules for filling and uploading (verified in our guide):</strong></p><ul class="checklist"><li>Print it and fill it by hand in a blue or black ball pen, in clear letters; no cutting or overwriting — if a mistake happens, fill a fresh print.</li><li>At the marked place below, <strong>put your signature and the date</strong> — an unsigned declaration is not valid.</li><li>Take a clear photo/scan of the filled form — the whole page in frame, edges not cut off.</li><li>Upload happens <strong>only in JPEG format and under 100KB</strong> — the portal does not accept it as a PDF.</li><li>Every application carries a <strong>separate</strong> declaration with that service’s details — one copy will not do for three certificates.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>The route — from download to upload</h2><figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/forms-download-flow.svg" alt="The forms route — choose the service, get the official form, fill and sign it, scan as JPEG, upload with the application (diagram)" /><figcaption>The form’s journey — four steps</figcaption></figure><ol><li><strong>Choose the service:</strong> first decide which certificate the application is for — the declaration is filled with that service’s details.</li><li><strong>Get the form:</strong> download the format from the official portal edistrict.up.gov.in, or use the format provided during the eSathi application.</li><li><strong>Fill and sign:</strong> fill it clearly by hand and add signature + date.</li><li><strong>Scan and upload:</strong> make a JPEG (under 100KB), upload it in the application’s “self-declaration” section, and zoom the preview to check it.</li></ol></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Service by service — which form/paper goes with each</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Form / declaration arrangement</th><th>Detailed guide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Income Certificate</td><td>Online application on eSathi + self-declaration (JPEG). A wrong declaration format is a common cause of rejection.</td><td><a href="/en/income-certificate/">Income Certificate guide</a></td></tr><tr><td>Caste Certificate</td><td>Online application + Aadhaar, photo and declaration (JPEG) uploads. If the father’s caste certificate exists, attach it — verification becomes easier.</td><td><a href="/en/caste-certificate/">Caste Certificate guide</a></td></tr><tr><td>Domicile Certificate</td><td>Online application + signed declaration (JPEG only) and proof of residence — voter ID, electricity bill, old documents.</td><td><a href="/en/domicile-certificate/">Domicile Certificate guide</a></td></tr><tr><td>Birth Certificate</td><td>Instead of a separate declaration, the hospital birth report / discharge summary is the main paper; for late registration, a declaration/affidavit explaining the delay. Files under 100KB, JPEG or PDF.</td><td><a href="/en/birth-certificate/">Birth Certificate guide</a></td></tr><tr><td>Death Certificate</td><td>Death-report documents + in case of late registration, a declaration explaining the delay. Files under 100KB, JPEG or PDF.</td><td><a href="/en/death-certificate/">Death Certificate guide</a></td></tr><tr><td>EWS Certificate</td><td>Online application; the family income and asset details are filled inside the application itself — before applying, check the format and financial year given in the notice of the recruitment/admission you are applying for.</td><td><a href="/en/ews-certificate/">EWS Certificate guide</a></td></tr><tr><td>Parivar Register Nakal</td><td>This is not a portal-form service — the application is given to the Gram Panchayat Secretary; for changing an entry after a birth, death or marriage, a copy of the related certificate is attached.</td><td><a href="/en/parivar-register-nakal/">Parivar Register Nakal guide</a></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">Note — the full list of documents for each service is in its own guide. This table takes only the “where does the form come from, and how” question, so no time is wasted hunting for forms.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>The “application form” is actually filled online</h2><p>Many people keep hunting for a separate PDF form for every service — while in the eDistrict system the application is filled as an <strong>online form</strong> on the eSathi portal: you log in with Citizen Login, choose the service, fill the details, upload the documents and receive the application number (SRN). The only regular paper form is the declaration (and, in special situations, an affidavit when a department demands one). So the correct answer to searches like “income certificate form pdf” is not some unknown PDF but the right order — first understand <a href="/en/login-registration/">eSathi Login &amp; Registration</a>, then apply online as per the service’s guide. If you apply through a CSC (Jan Seva Kendra), the same form is filled online there too — the centre’s operator performs the same steps on your behalf; there too, do not forget to carry the signed declaration’s JPEG copy with you. Precautions for centres are in our <a href="/en/csc-guide/">CSC guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Form mistakes that stall applications</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Result / prevention</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No signature on the declaration</td><td>The declaration loses its legal meaning — the most common cause of rejection. Check signature and date before uploading.</td></tr><tr><td>Declaration uploaded as a PDF</td><td>The portal accepts the declaration only as a JPEG. Convert the photo/scan to JPEG and keep it under 100KB.</td></tr><tr><td>Blurred or cut-off photo</td><td>If it cannot be read at verification, the application comes back. Photograph the whole page in frame, in good light, and zoom the preview to check.</td></tr><tr><td>Old/wrong format taken from another website</td><td>Attach only the official portal edistrict.up.gov.in format — formats can change.</td></tr><tr><td>The same declaration attached to every application</td><td>Each service’s declaration is different. Fill a fresh declaration with the details of the service you are applying for.</td></tr><tr><td>Declaration details differ from the documents</td><td>A declaration is a legal statement — the Lekhpal/Revenue Inspector checks it at verification. Write only what is true and matches your documents.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>After submitting the application, keep checking its status with the application number — the method is in our <a href="/en/status-verification/">Status &amp; Verification guide</a>. Once the certificate is issued, the download route is in the <a href="/en/blog/esathi-download/">eSathi Download</a> article.</p></section>
